workedExamples.solution_text:
(a) At $t=0$:
$\begin{aligned}86&=22+Ae^0\\86&=22+A\\A&=64.\end{aligned}$
Here $A$ is the initial difference from room temperature.
At $t=6$:
$\begin{aligned}54&=22+64e^{6k}\\32&=64e^{6k}\\e^{6k}&=\dfrac12\\6k&=\ln\dfrac12\\k&=-\dfrac{\ln2}{6}.\end{aligned}$
(b) Using the exact value of $k$:
$\begin{aligned}T(12)&=22+64e^{12k}\\&=22+64\left(\dfrac12\right)^2\\&=38\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.\end{aligned}$
(c) Since $A>0$ and $k<0$, the curve decreases from $(0,86)$ towards $T=22$ from above, becoming less steep. It never reaches room temperature in this model.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=70mm,height=52mm,axis lines=left,xlabel={$t$},ylabel={$T$},xmin=0,xmax=32,ymin=0,ymax=100,xtick=\empty,ytick={22,86},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},clip=false]
\addplot[black,dashed,domain=0:30] {22};
\addplot[black,thick,->,samples=100,domain=0:30] {22+64*exp(-ln(2)*x/6)};
\addplot[black,only marks,mark=*] coordinates {(0,86)};
\path (axis cs:19,22) node[below,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {$T=22$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a) The limiting charge gives $P=100$. At $t=0$:
$\begin{aligned}20&=100+A\\A&=-80.\end{aligned}$
Thus $Q(t)=100-80e^{kt}$, where $k<0$.
(b) At $t=4$:
$\begin{aligned}60&=100-80e^{4k}\\-40&=-80e^{4k}\\e^{4k}&=\dfrac12\\k&=-\dfrac{\ln2}{4}.\end{aligned}$
(c) Since $A<0$ and $k<0$, the curve increases from $(0,20)$ towards $Q=100$ from below, becoming less steep. The model approaches full charge without reaching it at a finite time.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=70mm,height=52mm,axis lines=left,xlabel={$t$},ylabel={$Q$},xmin=0,xmax=22,ymin=0,ymax=120,xtick=\empty,ytick={20,100},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},clip=false]
